Ok, I will:

1. I will implement primality testing by checking divisibility up to the square root, handling two and three as special cases and returning false for zero and one.
2. I will compute prime factors by removing factors of two, then testing odd candidates only up to the remaining square root, and appending any leftover prime.
3. I will build divisors from the grouped prime factors by multiplying existing divisors by each prime power and sorting the final list.
4. I will calculate Euler's totient from the distinct prime factors using the product formula, with explicit results for zero and one.

Here are the diffs for those changes:

```diff
--- prime_toolkit.rs
+++ prime_toolkit.rs
@@ ... @@
-pub fn is_prime(n: u64) -> bool {
-    unimplemented!()
-}
-
-pub fn prime_factors(n: u64) -> Vec<u64> {
-    unimplemented!()
-}
