1. I treat each codeword as an unsigned byte whose bit 0 is Hamming position 1 and whose bit 7 is position 8, so a tiny helper reads any position.
2. For encoding I place the four nibble bits in data positions 3, 5, 6 and 7, derive the three parity bits from their standard groups, then add an overall parity bit so every valid codeword has an even number of set bits.
3. To inspect a codeword I recompute those three group parities into a three-bit syndrome and compare it with the overall parity: clean, position 8, a single flipped position, or an uncorrectable double error.
4. Repairing flips the reported bit, and decoding repairs first and then reads the data bits back; out-of-range nibbles and uncorrectable codewords raise IllegalArgumentException.

